# Vitamin-C detective: the reaction ledger behind a disappearing colour
#
# Iodine held in dilute Lugol solution makes starch blue-black. Ascorbic acid
# reduces iodine and is oxidised to dehydroascorbic acid, so the colour clears.
# Protect clothes and the table, wear eye protection, and never taste a mixture
# that has been used as a laboratory sample.
register lv2

# The positive colour control: starch plus the same Lugol dose.
add v1 water 100mL
add v1 cornstarch 1g
add v1 lugol_solution_1_percent 2mL
look v1

# Change one thing: add a known amount of vitamin C before the iodine. The
# iodine is consumed while vitamin C remains, so the blue-black positive does
# not appear. At this dose the current broad oxidizer/reducer safety screen may
# print a warning written for concentrated reagents; it is deliberately
# conservative and is not a quantitative risk assessment of this dilute cup.
new beaker
add v2 water 100mL
add v2 cornstarch 1g
add v2 ascorbic_acid 0.01mol
add v2 lugol_solution_1_percent 2mL
inspect v2

# The ledger names dehydroascorbic acid even though the current appearance
# renderer answers the colourless cup with a generic "nothing visible" line.
# That is a presentation boundary, not permission to invent a colour event.
# This is also not a calibrated food assay: a real juice has an uncertain
# vitamin concentration and other reducing compounds, so colour alone does not
# justify a nutrition-label result.
